d comply with the new regulations by  buying new muffler  this unit here is $15,000 that truck the  way it sits today because the death date  on it is probably worth $5,000 $5,000  that's the value of the rims and tires  the only parts of the truck worth  anything to a potential buyer you're  spending and whitney says the mufflers  are unreliable you are gambling with  with your business  new carb rules are basically going to  put me out of business  in the world we live in you can't really  separate science from politics and  that's very very unfortunate the money  that researchers need to do their  research comes from agencies they'd have  to generate that money on the basis of  frequently public fears  professor Robert Phelan is co-director  of the air pollution health effects lab  at UC Irvine so we see public fears  being exploited in order to maintain the  funding to agencies falen notes that  today California's air is much cleaner  than it was in the 60s and 70s and he  credits carb regulations with helping  make that happen but few people realize  how much air quality has improved so  it's very difficult for a researcher to  come out and say something is a less  lesser risk than the public police the  agency has sort of taken on a life of  its own they're going to be on the  forefront of regulations whether the in  fact the regulations are science-based  or not instruments that research that  has come out since his 2005 study  generally agrees with his study it's  actually consistent with the great body  of evidence yet UCLA did not come to n  streams defense in fact in February 2010  UCLA officials told him that after 34  years at the University he was now out  of a job what's happened to dr. instrum  I think is bad news for scientists in  general the engine of innovation  progress and ideas depends at a very  deep and fundamental level on academic  freedom  Adam Kissel of fire the foundation for  individual rights in education which is  defending dr. instrum in his fight to  keep his job if dr. Antrim loses his job  because he has expressed his academic  freedom then it's a message to other  researchers that you better not rock the  boat because you might be next  we asked UCLA officials to come on  camera but they declined opting instead  to send an email which says they can't  comment on quote a confidential personal  matter however dr. instrum has waived  his confidentiality and privacy rights  in this matter his record is open for  review by anyone UCLA has made various  claims against n strim for instance they  said there was no longer money to fund  his position but later the money  materialized they said his research was  not aligned with the mission of his  department but then that charge was  dropped my guess is they didn't really  think this through very clearly and  didn't have a specific evidence the  environmental regulation machine is  powerful in California when dr. instrum  went up against that machine he was  retaliated against retaliated against  says Kissel for publishing politically  incorrect research and for shaking  things up at carb Mary Nichols the head  of carb was also a professor at UCLA as  is John Froines who lost his powerful  position at carb after n streams whistle  blowing in fact Froines is the most  powerful person in instrument apartment  and one who voted to lay him off and I  have felt very intimidated by this  process and to me the worst thing is to  not consider the cost of meeting new  regulations people are unemployed the  economy is hurt that hits public health  very very hard  I took my hard-earned money and invested  it in something that was supposed to  make me money and last me a lifetime  now I wake up the lifetimes over  there's gonna be an awful lot of truck  drivers looking for work Mary Nichols  declined our interview request  in an April 4th hearing will determine  whether dr. instrum keeps his job but  the final decision rests with UCLA  Chancellor Jeanne block f